# ============================================================================
# benchmarks/size_watch/startup_floor.hls — Stage 139: the tracked size
# suite, workload 1 of 5 — the FLOOR.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The binary-size floor every Halis native program stands on: the C
# runtime's static image, the stdio plumbing, the soft-real-time
# wiring, the panic paths — everything a `hlc` emission links in
# before the program's own first statement. The body allocates
# nothing and calls nothing beyond one println, so a growth on any
# axis here means the RUNTIME got heavier, not the program — the
# first thing a binary-size dashboard must be able to say, and the
# number a user's smallest tool pays in full.
#
# The hls-size suite convention (Stage 139): every workload is
# deterministic, allocates bounded memory and prints one checksum
# line — the suites are compiled and linked, never benchmarked, so
# their own output exists to prove the emission is alive, not to be
# timed.
# ============================================================================

fn main() -> int uses IO {
    println("floor")
    return 0
}
